      "message": "In `readline()`, you correctly catch `OSError` when calling `os.read()`. However, you immediately treat *any* `OSError` as a fatal disconnect. If the underlying file descriptor is non-blocking (or if `select()` experiences a spurious wakeup), `os.read()` can throw an `OSError` with `EAGAIN` or `EWOULDBLOCK`. Treating this as an `EOFError` will prematurely crash the test.\n\n* **Suggestion:** Check the error number and safely continue if it\u0027s a transient blocking error.\n\n```python\nimport errno\n\n# ... inside readline() ...\n        try:\n            ch \u003d os.read(fd, 1)\n        except OSError as e:\n            if e.errno in (errno.EAGAIN, errno.EWOULDBLOCK):\n                continue\n            if line:\n                return bytes(line)\n            raise EOFError(\"Console disconnected\") from e\n\n```",
